**Definition 14.2 (activation material and initialization).** At activation
$A$, the seed recipient and retained-ingress owner actually authorize the
needed copies. The source token stays with the producer. Asset copies preserve source-ready
public heads as follows. Before moving a public source head $i$, serialize
its current distance $h$ by RD; on L append 0 and stop, otherwise ML;
Append(1) and repeat into owned scratch. This constructs $U(h)$ and leaves
that head at L, with $5h+4$ body occurrences. Copy the saved word into the
manifest head table before reusing scratch. After asset copying, restore
it by Home(i), then one RD(saved); MR(i); MR(saved) per leading 1 and a
final RD(0). Positioning of the saved word is the existing scratch/field
scan. Only public seed/frame-copy heads need this; no private head is read
or moved. Tokens borrowed for these actual copies return to their canonical
source-ready owners by explicit TOKs. The eight unused
verifier port tokens are transferred to $O$ by the capture controller
$L$'s eight actual TOKs, counted once. Old
boundary cells exist from original boot; their heads are positioned by
Home/Tail. Append the bank and frame-stream sentinels, then install/import
records with the §11 and §13 append/descriptor routines.

Scan the owner's actual complete old frame prefix to construct its count
$j_A$ in unary. Preserve its full original material, seed and origins by
authorized bitwise copies into port 37. The copied manifest also contains
$D_0$, the canonical public boot material and $D_1$'s actual code-capture frame and the fixed capture-controller encoding.
The literal descriptions of $D_0$ and $L$ are source-independent compiler
constants: $L$ does not contain $D_1$, so the compiler first constructs
$L$, then embeds those two finite words in $D_1$ by finite chains of
literal appends. Their emission into manifest fields is paid per bit.
It does not read the overwritten control store or assume those old code
bits have an ordinary read port. Only $D_1$'s own description uses the
prior actual capture; it is never embedded in $D_1$. Other fixed finite
boot constants use the same literal-emission rule. Variable public $H,\xi$
and actual source/seed/frame descriptors come from their already acquired
public backing by authorized copies, not compiler literals.
The record order is fixed: captured new-description frame, old public
initialization, old description, capture-controller description, acquired
seed, old frames in chronological order with each referenced context in
its source-frame order, their copy-origin fields, and the saved public-head
table in ascending port order. Each top-level physical field is one binary
block followed by F on 37; it has the same Base/length/full descriptor/seal
construction as the captured code block. Its internal variable lists and
tuples are length framed with Definition 12.1's wire grammar, emitted by
the native unary/copy routines before bank initialization. The captured
code field includes its 101 header and real length; the old-frame field
is the explicit encoded list, with 0 for no old frames. Its frame count
$j_A$ is constructed during that list scan. Repeated
material references are copied in that same order rather than resolved by
an external dictionary. Each copied finite source field uses its existing
actual descriptor, full positioning and bitwise copy graph. Only completed
owned source blocks enter this manifest. Its framed records seal only after these copies. No new $j_A$ input or
primitive-meter numeral is supplied. If the retained old material is not
already local to $O$, its actual channel copies are part of installation.
The source's no-update acquired seed is reused; no additional TagRead or
private-latch access is introduced.

Thus before full-prefix import the actual port-37 tape has precisely
Lemma 13.2a's shape, with captured code as $w_0$ and the fixed subsequent
fields in this order. The old-frame list is one field with an internal
variable-length list, not a variable number of top-level fields. Its
empty case is the word 0; any other legitimately empty top-level field
still has its own terminating F. Neither case changes another field's
index. The captured field is the nonempty word
$101\,U(d_1)\,\Delta(D_1)$ of length $2d_1+4$. Its Base is one and its
first actual descriptor has ordinal zero, as used by Definition 13.3's
version pointer. Later fields, including the complete saved-head table,
are finished and sealed before import. Their source-head restorations
are paid native work; import does not stand in for those restorations.

The activation record says: after old acquisition and exactly these
$j_A$ complete original frames, take the declared Install CTL, execute
these two bootstraps, code capture, asset-copy and initialization graphs,
and thereafter use $D_1$.
Its old boundary ordinal is computed by Boundary replay of this actual
old backing, not read from a private meter. This records the one actual
activation choice at its complete-frame position. The actual choice to
activate there is additional control information. Omitting this record
would not determine which old Ready led to installation.

There is no self-description fixed-point assumption: native $D_1$ does
not contain its own bit string. Its public description is acquired by the actually installed capture
controller before recovery runs. Step reads that owned ordinary data.
When simulating either bootstrap it supplies the prescribed $E(L)$ bits
or constructs $E(D_1)$ by the explicit serialization of its actual captured
$\Delta(D_1)$ frame. Its capture-phase INs use that frame's literal bits. The bootstrap
recipe is fixed finite code; the service description is acquired data.
Initialization first constructs the initial empty bank by Proposition
11.4's native prelude, then runs ManifestPrefix and parses its exact fields
by Definition 13.1. Only after that full-prefix scan may staging append
other blocks to 37. Initialization creates the service archive records,
installs the finite service-token state bit by
bit, executes the receiver-port handoff to $C$, hands the service token to $C$, and executes the first installed Ready CTL.

